# The reviewer's merged baseline, wherever a copy of it lands. `feedback-phase-3/`
# below already covers the canonical copy, but a second one was dropped at the
# repo root and `git add -A` swept it into a commit: 1.3 GB, five files over
# GitHub's 100 MB hard limit, and an unpushable branch. Match the name itself
# rather than one path, since the next copy will land somewhere new again.
merged*/
**/merged*/

# Data policy: big data stays out of git; the repo commits code + docs only.
# The net-new deliverable (output/) grew large once real sources landed, so it
# now ships in the delivery archive, regenerable on any machine via `ark export`.
# NEVER commit (GitHub rejects >100MB files); big input/intermediate/output data:
feedback-phase-1/
feedback-external-phase-2/
feedback-phase-3/
feedback-phase-4/
legacy-data/
/data/
output/
*.duckdb
*.duckdb.wal
*.parquet
*.warc
*.warc.gz

# One folder per submitted round, so a new round stops overwriting the last. The
# tarball is gigabytes and stays out; the small records of what was sent stay in,
# because losing the claim is worse than losing the bytes, and the bytes are
# regenerable from the commit named in MANIFEST.txt.
submissions/**/*.tar.gz


# Email drafts and personal correspondence: never ship inside the delivery
# archive, which packages every tracked file via `git archive HEAD`.
private/

# Research handoff and agent prompt: working notes, not deliverables. They now live
# under legacy/notes/, and that subtree stays ignored: these eight files have never
# been in git, one of them breaks house style, and docs/notes.md records what
# happened the last time private working prose travelled inside a delivery archive.
# Archiving them must not be the act that commits them.
legacy/notes/
handoff-research.md
handback-research.md
prompt-research-agent.md
handback-sources-A.md
handback-sources-B.md
